{% set local_problem %}
<p>Huntsville sits on some of the most unforgiving soil in Alabama for drainage. Madison County's red clay doesn't absorb water well, and much of the city is influenced by the Tennessee River watershed — meaning high water tables and ground that's already saturated during wet seasons.</p>

<p>When you combine that with 55+ inches of annual rainfall and intense spring storms, simple downspout extensions don't cut it. Surface extensions move water a few feet from your foundation, but on clay soil, that water just pools there — finding the path of least resistance, which is often your basement or crawlspace.</p>

<p>We see this constantly in Huntsville: homes in Jones Valley with wet basements despite "working" gutter systems. Hampton Cove houses with foundation cracks from years of water pooling. Monte Sano properties where slope should help, but water still finds its way back toward the home.</p>

<p>Underground drains solve this permanently. Instead of moving water a few feet and hoping for the best, they route thousands of gallons far enough away that it can never threaten your foundation. For Huntsville's unique combination of clay soil, high water tables, and heavy rainfall, it's often the only real solution.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Underground drains are buried pipes that connect to your downspouts and carry water through a sealed, sloped channel to a discharge point far from your home. The water exits at a predetermined location — typically at the property edge, into a storm drain (where permitted), or into a dry well that allows gradual absorption.</p>

<p>We use 4-inch corrugated or solid PVC pipe, depending on your specific application and soil conditions. The system is designed with proper slope (typically 1-2% grade) to ensure water flows away continuously rather than pooling inside the pipe. Pop-up emitters or daylight exits release water at the discharge point.</p>

<p>Unlike French drains (which collect groundwater that's already in the soil), underground gutter drains specifically handle roof runoff before it reaches the ground. They work in conjunction with your gutter system to create complete water management — from the moment rain hits your roof until it's safely away from your home.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've installed underground drainage systems throughout Huntsville and Madison County for over 20 years. We understand the soil conditions, the Tennessee River watershed's influence, the water table variations across different neighborhoods, and the drainage patterns specific to this area.</p>

